  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A resource-rich cavern system in the Nightshelf interior, adjacent to the corruption source known as the Forgotten Shore. Locally called "the Umbra," though the formal designation persists in administrative filings. The site yields fire-cores of exceptional quality alongside deep-seam relics of uncertain origin, and has been contested ground between the Wake-Singers — an orca shaman cult devoted to waking a dead god — and the Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, a Nightshelf merchant guild, for as many seasons as either has held it. The cavern system thrums with corrupted magic from multiple sources, and its deeper passages exhibit anomalous properties that resist conventional classification.
    
    ## Discovery
    
    ☽'Iarlocias the Silent, a Nightshelf mystic later known for breaking with the Communion, discovered the site during the Clever Ice Age. Nightshelf records — which I have cross-referenced against administrative ice from the period — note that ☽'Iarlocias brought out samples and ceased speaking about what lay deeper. The Nightshelf records are characteristically precise about what was recovered and characteristically silent about what was encountered. This is their way of telling you something went wrong.
    
    ## Resource Extraction and Hazards
    
    The cavern's fire-veins produce cores that burn at double standard duration. The highest-grade specimens burn purple-spectrum and exceed the thermal output of any other Nightshelf source. However, the ash carries a trace-signature making bare-flipper handling inadvisable past forty burns, and miners working the deep seams return — the reports are consistent on this point — *quieter*.
    
    Extraction output has declined approximately 31%, with active crews reduced from twenty-two to fourteen across three shifts. Miners below the breach line rotate at accelerated intervals. These figures come from a field assessment by the E'frostlings 4th Terrace Bureau, which recommended against acquisition — a rare moment of institutional wisdom.
    
    ## The Breach and Corruption
    
    A secondary chamber bears the mark of the Thron∴mbra Fracta, a geological rupture of disputed origin. The breach weeps a substance that is not water. Corruption spreading from the Forgotten Shore has reached the chamber floor.
    
    Relics recovered from deeper seams are catalogued by resonance-weight rather than origin — a classification method that tells you more about the cataloguers' uncertainty than about the relics. Prolonged proximity produces acoustic phenomena, cold-burn discoloration, and behavioural changes sufficient that couriers refuse standard transport. Guild-escorted passage through the Thrall☽ Vavmastius corridor remains the sole delivery route.
    
    ## Notable Artifacts and Manifestations
    
    Augur ☽'Milucaum~ Shadow-bane recovered the volcanic glass blade Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ from these depths — an artifact radiating the suppressive silence later classified as Hush☽ Twilight, an anti-source technique targeting aurora-light itself. The upper warrens host presences that Nightshelf sources describe as "watchers from the deep." When the Thaw-Paths Medallion, an aurora-glass artifact tied to the Communion, was brought into the passages, those presences reportedly shifted from ambient unease to focused attention.
    
    Warning-marks in Old Pressure-Tongue line the main tunnel. Someone scratched them out.
    
    ## Contested Control
    
    Three factions have held this site in as many seasons. None repaired the collapsed eastern tunnel. The graffiti there — names, warnings, a child's drawing of something with too many eyes — remains.
